# Agent Guidelines & Quality Architecture (Vanilla Web Profile)

This repository enforces strict, deterministic quality gates, W3C web standards, and Clean Architecture boundaries for native web applications. Every AI agent (Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor, Copilot, or Spec Kit subagents) working in this workspace must adhere to these non-negotiable standards.

---

## 1. Clean Architecture Boundaries (Vertical Slices)

Code is organized into vertical feature slices under `src/features/<slice>/` and shared cross-cutting modules under `src/core/`:

```
src/features/<slice>/
├── domain/           # Pure business logic: entities, value objects, domain invariants (zero external dependencies)
├── application/      # Use cases, application services, repository ports (interfaces)
├── infrastructure/   # Data adapters, HTTP clients, persistence implementations
└── ui/               # Native DOM controllers, custom elements, web components, local styles
```

### Unidirectional Dependency Rule
- **Domain** cannot import from **Application**, **Infrastructure**, or **UI**.
- **Application** cannot import from **Infrastructure** or **UI**.
- **Infrastructure** cannot import from **UI**.
- **UI** cannot directly import from **Infrastructure** (must depend on application ports).
- *Violations will trigger fatal errors in `vp check` / Oxlint architectural boundary linting.*

---

## 2. Centralized Typed Environment Configuration

- **Never** access `process.env` or `import.meta.env` directly in application code.
- All environment variables must be declared, validated (e.g. Zod), typed, and exported from `src/config/env.ts`.
- Direct access is strictly blocked by `.specify/scripts/bash/check-env-config.sh`.

---

## 3. Styling & DOM Rules (Vanilla Web)

- **Pure Vanilla CSS & Design Tokens**:
  - All class names in HTML or DOM templates must correspond to declared CSS rules in project stylesheets or design tokens (`tokens.css`). Ghost classes will fail `.specify/scripts/bash/check-ghost-classes.sh`.
  - Prefer semantic HTML elements (`<header>`, `<main>`, `<nav>`, `<article>`, `<button>`) and accessible landmarks.
  - No React or JSX dependencies: presentation code uses native DOM manipulation or standard Web Components.
- **Function Line Limits**: Business logic functions (`domain`, `application`, `infrastructure`) must not exceed 50 lines or cyclomatic complexity 10. Max depth: 3. Max parameters: 4.
- Test suites (`**/*.test.*`, `tests/**`) are legitimately exempt from function line limits.

---

## 4. Quality Gate Integrity & Real Execution (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- **Gate configuration is human-owned.** You MUST NOT modify `vite.config.ts`, `package.json` quality scripts, `.specify/scripts/bash/*`, or `.specify/extensions.yml` to make a failing check pass.
- **Never suppress violations**: Prohibited from adding `eslint-disable`, `oxlint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, or skipping tests.
- **Mandatory Dependencies (`node_modules`)**: You MUST install dependencies (`vp install` or `npm install`) via terminal whenever `package.json` is created or modified. Checking tasks without a functioning `node_modules/` directory is prohibited.
- **Sandbox Boundary Safety (`node_modules/` and `.git/`)**: NEVER use `read_file`, `list_directory`, or file tools on `node_modules/` or `.git/`. In modern package managers (pnpm, npm, vite-plus), `node_modules` contains symlinks pointing outside the workspace sandbox, which triggers fatal environment errors; `.git/` accesses violate workspace security policies. Manage packages and git exclusively via terminal commands (`npm ls`, `npm install`, `vp check`, `git status`, `git diff`).
- **Build File Security Policy (`package.json`)**: NEVER use `replace` or `write_file` on `package.json` (blocked by security policy for build configuration files). To update scripts or configuration, use terminal commands: `npm pkg set scripts.<name>="..."`.
- **Terminal Execution Required**: You MUST execute `.specify/scripts/bash/check-quality-gate.sh` via the terminal after completing each task in `tasks.md`. Mid-feature it is EXPECTED to exit non-zero — it runs the whole test suite and the build, so tasks not yet written guarantee a red gate. That red gate is your progress signal; it is not a reason to stop, and it is not a reason to skip the check.
- **Ledger Reconciliation (tick only on a green gate)**: Marking `- [x]` while the gate exits non-zero is a fatal violation of Quality Gate Integrity. That rule also means no checkbox *can* be ticked mid-feature, so on the first full-gate success you MUST reconcile `tasks.md` in one pass: tick every task that is now demonstrably done and print `task id -> justification` for each (the file, test or gate result that makes it true). Leave unchecked — with a one-line reason — any task you cannot justify; a human decides. `Polish` / verification tasks are ticked only when the gate they describe actually passed in this run (full test suite, visual/accessibility audit, bundle budget). A finished feature with an empty ledger fails `gate_tasks` and spends a repair window on bookkeeping.
- **Imperative Pipeline Enforcement**: Spec Kit runs automated quality gates (`gate_quality`, `gate_bundle`, `gate_contracts`, `gate_tasks`, `gate_verdict`) as non-agent shell steps with an automated self-healing repair window.
- Resolve every violation by fixing the **application code**.

---

## 5. Subagent-Driven Execution Architecture (Anti-Context Drift)

To prevent cognitive degradation, context drift, sycophancy, and timeouts on large feature sets:
- **Ephemeral Subagent Delegation**: When executing multi-task phases in `tasks.md`, the orchestrating agent MUST dispatch individual tasks to fresh, isolated worker subagents (e.g. `@generalist`).
- **Context Isolation**: Each worker subagent receives ONLY the task description, the target file path, and relevant domain contracts. It does NOT inherit the entire conversation history.
- **Mandatory Quality Gate per Task**: The worker subagent MUST execute `bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-quality-gate.sh` via terminal and verify exit code 0 before concluding.
- **Single-Turn Lifecycle**: Upon task completion and gate verification, the subagent returns a concise 3-line status to the orchestrator and its context is disposed of immediately.

---

## 6. Verification Commands & Machine-Generated Evidence

Run these deterministic gates before declaring any task complete. Each gate emits structured telemetry to `.specify/telemetry.jsonl` and records machine proof artifacts in `specs/<feature>/evidence/`:

```bash
# Core gate: formatting (Oxfmt), linting (Oxlint), strict typing, architecture boundaries, and test suite
bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-quality-gate.sh

# CSS class integrity & ghost class detection
bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-ghost-classes.sh

# Performance & bundle budget (max 250KB per chunk, max 1000KB total)
bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-bundle-budget.sh

# Deterministic project bootstrap: git init + baseline commit
bash .specify/scripts/bash/bootstrap-project-env.sh

# Task completion ledger (fails while tasks.md has unchecked items)
bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-tasks-complete.sh

# Machine-readable final verdict (fails unless evidence/verification.json says "pass")
bash .specify/scripts/bash/check-verification-verdict.sh

# Never pass these to make a gate green without explicit human approval:
#   --allow-no-tests  --allow-incomplete  --allow-missing  --allow-foreign-git

# Review recorded telemetry: every workflow step, model, subagent and gate outcome
bash .specify/scripts/bash/emit-telemetry.sh --import-state --summary
```
